    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d2">

                <pb facs="0012" n="2" xml:id="pg2"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01/1–349</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Embassy to the
                            Department of State
                    </hi><anchor n="5" xml:id="d2-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                        calendar="gregorian" type="received-date" when="1949-01-03">undated</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>The Foreign Office state that, at first sight, they are inclined to think that
                    the question of giving or withholding recognition may not arise if the
                    Communists enter a coalition government. If His Majesty’s Ambassador and staff
                    are in Nanking, it is likely that they would do business with the new government
                    on a <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> basis.</p>
                <p>The Foreign Office say that they will examine the problem exhaustively as soon as
                    a coalition takes place.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d2-f1" n="5" type="source" xml:id="d2-f1-content">
                <p>Handed to the Chief of the Division of Chinese Affairs (Sprouse) by the First
                    Secretary of the British Embassy (Ford) on January 3.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
